(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2025-003"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2025-003","title":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 CS","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2025","num":3,"category":"advance","year":"2025","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/005_Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tThe number of integer solutions of equation <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74af1382a49083520d49.png\" /> is _____.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3 (3 - 3)","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t3 (3 - 3)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8c/8c292082c769531a436f.png\" /></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tIn an examination, the average marks of 4 girls and 6 boys is 24. Each of the girl has same marks while each of the boys has the same marks. If the marks of any girl is at most double the marks of any boy, but not less than the marks of any boy, then the number of possible distinct integer values of the total marks of 2 girls and 6 boys is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"21"},{"key":"B","html":"20"},{"key":"C","html":"22"},{"key":"D","html":"19"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/106f003c57025a1a0abb.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b5e35583a5078a816287.png\" /></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tNihal singh is seventh from left and Sourabh kumar is ninth from right. If there are 8 students between them then how many students are there in a row?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10"},{"key":"B","html":"14"},{"key":"C","html":"24"},{"key":"D","html":"23"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8ef202b9c48bf8afd726.png\" /></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tBased on the given paragraph, identify the meaning of the word stalking in the context of this paragraph : I once saw a lioness stalking a 3 month old kid. The ground was very open and the kid saw the lioness while she was still some distance away and started bleating whereon the lioness gave up her stalk and walked straight up to it.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"running threateningly"},{"key":"B","html":"targeting a victim"},{"key":"C","html":"moving about in circles"},{"key":"D","html":"moving quietly without being seen or heard"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)<br />\n\tStalking here refers to a silent approach of the lioness towards the 3 month old kid. She straight away walked up to the kind once the kid started crying (bleating) giving up all caution.</p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWe have 2 rectangular sheets of paper, M and N, of dimensions 6 cm × 1 cm each. Sheet M is rolled to form an open cylinder by bringing the short edges of the sheet together. Sheet N is cut into equal square patches and assembled to form the largest possible closed cube. Assuming the ends of the cylinder are closed by placing extra circular lids at the top and the bottom, then the ratio of the Surface area (internal and external both) of the cylinder to that of the cube is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/392d94efe67cfe1caecb.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf17703005843e002fcb.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e36ced920fc605462b5.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b391f9d604ee978f8531.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d3f8284ca8ba30dfb0c5.png\" /></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\t<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f064f7601cdbc47f59c2.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tDetails of prices of two items P and Q are presented in the above table. The ratio of cost of item P to cost of item Q is 2:5. Discount is calculated as the difference between the marked price and the selling price. The profit percentage is calculated as the ratio of the difference between selling price and cost price, to the cost price</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/00/00f48a9303734f9e9876.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe discount on item <em>Q</em>, as a percentage of its marked price, is________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"16"},{"key":"B","html":"16.5"},{"key":"C","html":"17"},{"key":"D","html":"17.5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf37261104fca5a14036.png\" /></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhat will be the final structure of paper when we follow steps 1, 2 and 3?</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/98/98dedcaf534faad0573a.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f9541b6fc1ae191803e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tSelect the option in which the given figure is embedded (rotation is NOT allowed).</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/2708541ddcf30749ac07.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5ad8224329b41c030c14.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ce9d9f9a1bbefc17bd3e.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b9/b97ab49e62d9ee73c5f6.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0a90253f059ab202b6e1.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/dac9c50472b6983ed55d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tThe board results are _________ any moment.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"expected"},{"key":"B","html":"launched"},{"key":"C","html":"compiled"},{"key":"D","html":"tabulated"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of these words is closest in meaning to the word provided?<br />\n\t<strong>ABHOR</strong></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"argue"},{"key":"B","html":"hate"},{"key":"C","html":"discover"},{"key":"D","html":"reveal"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\tto abhor means to feel intense or violent dislike, or to hate.</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tHow many number of strings of length exactly 3 does the language described by this regular expression (0 + 1)* 0(0 + 01)* contains?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t5<br />\n\t0 0 0<br />\n\t0 0 1<br />\n\t0 1 0<br />\n\t1 1 0<br />\n\t1 0 0</p>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following logic circuit shown in below figure, whose input are <em>f</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>f</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>f</em><sub>3</sub> and output is <em>f</em>. Given <em>f</em><sub>1</sub> = Σ<em>m</em>(1, 2, 4); <em>f</em><sub>2</sub> = Σ<em>m</em>(0, 1, 4, 7); <em>f</em><sub>3</sub> = Σ<em>m</em>(3, 6).</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5eee48f0fdeef6670412.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe minimum number of 2 × 1 multiplexer required to implement the Boolean function ‘<em>f</em> ’ of the above circuit without using any additional gates is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t2</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/bef97e0b978a7448a067.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a4/a42d5c998ded4789ef9a.png\" /></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tIn following grammar:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a08f338397b9699498ae.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"‘×’ and ‘/’ both are right associative."},{"key":"B","html":"‘/’ is right associative while ‘+’ and ‘×’ are left associative."},{"key":"C","html":"‘/’ and ‘+’ left associative while ‘×’ is right associative."},{"key":"D","html":"‘×’ and ‘/’ are left associative and ‘+’ is right associative."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)<br />\n\t&bull; Since S and A both follows left recursion so, &lsquo;&times;&rsquo; and &lsquo;/&rsquo; both are left associative.<br />\n\t&bull; &lsquo;+&rsquo; is right associative while &lsquo;&times;&rsquo; is right associative.</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tLet <em>G</em> be a cyclic group of order 72. Let g be a generator of the cyclic group <em>G</em>. What is the order of the element <em>g</em><sup>18</sup>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e7a6e15be6d708b9bde.png\" /></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the FA given below:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e6/e6f030e01961b4e40e59.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of the following is correct regular expression for the language accepted by this FA?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"00* + 11*"},{"key":"B","html":"0* + 1*"},{"key":"C","html":"00* + 00* 11*"},{"key":"D","html":"00* 1*"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/432b743ca4c63dfd351c.png\" /></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following relational algebra queries correct representation to retrieve Eid’s of female employees whose salary more than all male employees of the company over database relation Emp (<u>Eid</u> , Salary, Sex)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/40/407757d646a91776d3f1.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub> correct but not <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub> correct but not <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<em>Q</em><sub>2</sub> <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub> correct but not <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<em>Q</em><sub>2</sub> correct but not <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub></p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a8/a8b8fe76c3674ceb2369.png\" /></p>"},{"n":17,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWe insert the following keys into an empty AVL tree in the following order:<br />\n\t15, 19, 13, 8, 6, 7, 11, 12, 10, 4<br />\n\tWhich key will be the right child of the root after inserting all keys?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t12</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/4591da80d61a06726231.png\" /></p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following are not true?<br />\n\t<strong>I.</strong> Only gateway can be used to connect different protocol networks.<br />\n\t<strong>II.</strong> Gateway occurs at all layers.<br />\n\t<strong>III.</strong> Bridge and switch some times occur in network layer also.<br />\n\t<strong>IV.</strong> Repeater occurs at both physical layer and data link layer.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only I and IV"},{"key":"B","html":"Only II, III and IV"},{"key":"C","html":"Only III and IV"},{"key":"D","html":"Only I, II and III"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider an array of <em>n</em> element where each element is same. Based on this which of the following are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tAsymptotic running time bubble sort Ο(<em>n</em>).</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tAsymptotic running time of merge sort Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>).</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tAsymptotic running time selection sort Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>).</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tAsymptotic running time insertion sort Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>).</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)<br />\n\t&nbsp;</p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following solution to the synchronized problem is/are implemented in user mode?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Strict alternation"},{"key":"B","html":"Bakery algorithm"},{"key":"C","html":"Lock variable"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)<br />\n\tAll the three are implemented in the user mode. They don&rsquo;t require hardware support.<br />\n\tThese are software based solution and are therefore desirable solutions.</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tLet <em>G</em> be a graph with 14 edges and <em>G</em>′ be a graph with 22 edges then the number of vertices in graph <em>G</em> is ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4"},{"key":"B","html":"8"},{"key":"C","html":"7"},{"key":"D","html":"9"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6dd00f0ab4679ed227ea.png\" /></p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tLet G be any arbitrary grammar and G known to be LR(1). Assume that the number of error entries in LR(0) table is ‘a’ and number of error entries in SLR(1) table is ‘<em>b</em>’. What is the relation over a and <em>b</em>? [Assume an error is SR conflict or RR conflict]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e31bd0d6b35c72f3a9f1.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d4998ac6f3c3f5916db7.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f428e24998cb4753ccd8.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/4895d44b14832dc4c9bf.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/44/44296de95b66c33483f5.png\" /></p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhat is the output of the following program?</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9dc6ce5a98b1a1801d29.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"12"},{"key":"B","html":"11"},{"key":"C","html":"10"},{"key":"D","html":"13"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/61/611f98214395657013a3.png\" /></p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following language:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa9b1dc9f7f667b10fc6.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of above language is regular?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only I"},{"key":"B","html":"Only II"},{"key":"C","html":"Only I and II"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e34fb0bd67465d4bd8d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":25,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a hypothetical process contains 30 registers and 120 opcodes. Each instruction of the processor has four field namely as opcode, 2 register operands and 1 for direct addressing. The number of bits is used to represent direct addressing field when 32 bit of operand instruction size is used is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[15,15],"answerText":"15","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t15</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fd/fd90337999672ab8cb14.png\" /></p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Any DFS forest of an directed graph contain same number of trees.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If a topological sort exists for the vertices in a directed graph, then a DFS on the graph will produce no back edges.<br />\n\tWhich of the following option is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tNeither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\t&bull; The DFS forest may contain different numbers of tree (and tree edges) depending on the starting vertex and upon the order in which vertices are searched. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is false.</p>"},{"n":27,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tFor the circuit shown below:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d5a8a7ef05c27accd3c5.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tIf <em>A</em> = 11011010 and <em>B</em> = 11110000, then decimal equivalent of output will be (________)<sub>10</sub>.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[63,63],"answerText":"63","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t63</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c6700941e182a99df2fa.png\" /></p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider 12 processes sharing the CPU in a Round Robin scheduling algorithm. Assuming that each process switch takes 0.25 seconds, what must be the quantum size ‘<em>q</em>’ such that the overhead resulting from process switching is minimized but at the same time each process is guaranteed to get its turn at the CPU atleast every 80 seconds?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<em>q</em> ≤ 11 seconds</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<em>q</em> ≥ 11 seconds</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<em>q</em> ≥ 7 seconds</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<em>q</em> ≤ 7 seconds</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb73b4a03c52fca7543e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements with respect to application layer:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Datagram is the PDU (Protocol data unit) used.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : There is a fixed limit on the maximum size of data that it can pass on the TCP layer.<br />\n\tWhich of the following options is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tNeither of <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> or <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following schedule:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c5/c581fec3fa9a8852e263.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe above schedule is serializable as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0f/0f4eb60f67ed77d78b27.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2e1b97508285b6bc4e42.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1fd3ffa8ae69ae44a174.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"Not serializable"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/120effd2829537308968.png\" /></p>"},{"n":31,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a Direct cache with 64 blocks and block size of 16 bits. The block number where the byte address (1440)<sub>10</sub> mapped in the cache is ______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[16,16],"answerText":"16","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t16</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d44e730c0525bf84417.png\" /></p>"},{"n":32,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tThe following post-fix expression with single digit operands is evaluated using stack.</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3da3c2efd582e13ab9d0.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tNote that ∧ is the exponential operator. The maximum height of the stack ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t3</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b1ddac82cb67adee753.png\" /></p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider an array A with is an array based binary heap with 70 nodes. Find the index of the first leaf node in the array if the index of the array starts from 1.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"64"},{"key":"B","html":"33"},{"key":"C","html":"36"},{"key":"D","html":"63"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a07259c34f5d8830fbb0.png\" /></p>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a computer system with 34 bit virtual addressing and 48 bit physical addressing and page size is 16 KB. Each page table entry contain 2 valid bit, 3 protection bit and 1 permission bit. The approximate size of the page table when virtual memory uses single level paging _______ (MB).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t5</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ed/ed62b4597350e327f95d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t(a) Every candidate key of a relation is simple, then it will be always be in 2NF.<br />\n\t(b) If every attribute is a prime attribute in the relation then it will always be in 3NF.<br />\n\t(c) A prime attribute of a relation schema R is an attribute that appears in all candidate keys of R.<br />\n\t(d) BCNF decompositions are always lossless as well as dependency preserving.<br />\n\tWhich of the above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b)<br />\n\t&bull; If every candidate key will be simple, then there will be no partial dependency, hence relation will be in 2NF. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; If every attribute is prime, then there will be no transitive dependency, hence the relation will always be in 3NF. So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; A prime attribute of a relation schema R is an attribute that appears in some candidate key of R. So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; BCNF decompositions are lossless but need not be dependency preserving. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":36,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider following grammar:<br />\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46f48b7f224f3ea13ffa.png\" /><br />\n\tThe difference between internal nodes and leaf node for the parse tree for 6 × 5 + 10 × 7 – 8 – 9 __________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[7,7],"answerText":"7","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t7</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/82c78155d67058952257.png\" /></p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Relative mode is the addressing mode which can be used to write code in which reallocation is done at run time.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Indirect addressing through register can be used to access local variables.<br />\n\tWhich of the following option is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tNeither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)<br />\n\t&bull; Relative addressing mode and based addressing modes are suitable for program reallocation at run time.<br />\n\t&bull; By first loading the operand&rsquo;s address in the register. Indirect addressing through register can be used to access global variables.</p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following code:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b0a612c5a35e01ac6d87.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of the following gives the best θ notation for the amount of time.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/06/0640a5d9e020c153ecc5.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/22775cb6d5d8e1170121.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7fa704339e11d9f0a849.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88db850d20bf8963f867.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/baf666feb46a879a9f57.png\" /></p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following two language:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a8/a87fb7f0859177ad6a10.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of the following option is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is REC</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is REC</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are REC</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above is REC"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d9a4993905e0b35d35f.png\" /></p>"},{"n":40,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tThe initial congestion window size over a TCP is 1. If slow start algorithm is used and the size of congestion window incremented by 1 whenever an ACK is received i.e. after first round trip time congestion window size is 2 segments. Assume that connection never leaves slow start. The number of RTT’s to send 3999 segments are ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t12</p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following C codes:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af857e149e99d219126e.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe root node of two Binary trees are passed as an argument to the given code. The code returns 1 if,</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both the trees are having same height if height is defined as the distance from root to the farthest leaf node."},{"key":"B","html":"Both the trees are Binary Search Trees."},{"key":"C","html":"Both the trees are mirror images of each other."},{"key":"D","html":"Both the trees have equal number of nodes."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\tThe code is checking whether the two trees are mirrors of each other.<br />\n\tIt returns 1 if the above property exist, 0 otherwise.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following processes, with the arrival time and the length of the CPU burst time given in msec</p>\n<p>\n\t(assume when burst time of two or more processes same priority given to lowest process id).</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5ed9e0f7846468eb93cc.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe scheduling algorithm used is pre-emptive longest job first. Find the difference between average turn around time and average waiting time of the these process is ________ msec. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6.25,6.25],"answerText":"6.25","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t6.25</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb7cbe13f09907b4eea6.png\" /></p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t(a) The complement of every Turing recognizable language is Turing recognizable.<br />\n\t(b) Turing recognizable language are closed under intersection.<br />\n\t(c) All CFL language which are not DCFL are ambiguous.<br />\n\t(d) The class of non-regular language is closed under complementation.<br />\n\tWhich of the above is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ad/ad4d8c7c28cf2d233c9d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tFor the 4 × 1 MUX shown below:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/794899cb26c0ca99c9ca.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe function <em>F</em> will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/8190535bb20181e14050.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a6d60e407437ad450a3d.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb8a7c2cd5f890fac8e2.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0995e0bc80d4c2da1cc.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b, c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45f668965d92ebd57c3c.png\" /></p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tA computer has a 256 KB, K-way set associative write-back data cache with block size of 32 byte. The address sent to the cache controller by the processor is of 32 bits. In addition to the address tag, each cache tag directory contains 2 valid bits and 1 modified bit. If 16 bits are used to address tag. What is the minimum value of K?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"8"},{"key":"D","html":"16"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d139990fe3a1ceb205c2.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/392801cf463de23482eb.png\" /></p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tIf a binary search tree is not allowed to have duplicates, there is more than one way to delete a node N from the tree when that node N has two children. One way involves choosing a replacement node from the left subtree of that node N. If this is done, which node are we looking for?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The largest node in the left subtree."},{"key":"B","html":"The smallest node in the left subtree."},{"key":"C","html":"The root of the left subtree."},{"key":"D","html":"Any node in the left subtree will do."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)<br />\n\tWhen a node has two children then we can do this:<br />\n\t&bull; First, we find the deletion node N (= the node that we want to delete).<br />\n\t&bull; Find the predecessor node of N.<br />\n\t&bull; Replace the content of node N with the content of the predecessor node.<br />\n\t&bull; Delete the predecessor node.<br />\n\tNote: Predecessor node = the node in the left subtree that has the maximum value.</p>"},{"n":47,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following language is having an equivalent deterministic PDA?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/870cf60dbe5bad276b07.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e3af6e1210566f9cb416.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fba7ccfbaaf957d154bd.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10197c856952bbbbe458.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b374a7dab8cbbef2a3a.png\" /></p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tA TCP server application is programmed to listen on port number P on host S. A TCP client is connected to the TCP server over the network. Consider that while the TCP connection was active, the server machine S crashed and rebooted. Assume that the client does not use the TCP keepalive timer.<br />\n\tWhich of the following behaviors is/are not possible?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If the client sends a packet after the server reboot, it will receive a FIN segment."},{"key":"B","html":"If the client was waiting to receive a packet, it may wait indefinitely."},{"key":"C","html":"The TCP server application on S can listen on P after reboot."},{"key":"D","html":"If the client sends a packet after the server reboot, it will receive a RST segment."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tIf <em>X</em> is a variable having Poisson’s distribution with mean <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/740992e8b46e459b135f.png\" /> such that <em>P</em>(2) = 9<em>P</em>(4) + 90<em>P</em>(6),then the value of mean <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/52/528c631e65e2606bc545.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"±1"},{"key":"B","html":"±2"},{"key":"C","html":"±3"},{"key":"D","html":"±4"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/44/442fbc075ac375a72e75.png\" /></p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"AVL tree prevent the skew structure."},{"key":"B","html":"Binary search tree always prevent the skew structure."},{"key":"C","html":"In min heap tree, the time take to find non-existent element is θ(1)."},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tIn AVL tree, the time taken to find a non-existent in the best case Ο(log <em>n</em>).</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6c31c4715b5a8eedd488.png\" /></p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following is incorrect for space allocation method used in disk?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Linked allocation method is free from external fragmentation."},{"key":"B","html":"Linked allocation method supports direct access."},{"key":"C","html":"Indexed allocation method is free from external fragmentation."},{"key":"D","html":"The pointer overhead of index block in indexed allocation is generally greater than the pointer overhead of linked allocation."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\tLinked allocation support sequential access of a block in a file.</p>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following circuit consisting of three <em>D</em>-flip flops.</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0f/0f166e93fda3799d3ffa.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tIf all the flip-flop were reset to 0 at initially, then the total number of distinct output states represented by the counter <em>ABC</em> is equal to ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t4</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e18ecd87743538a49811.png\" /></p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a relation <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9ddbfe29a0aa6b907b16.png\" /> on positive integer ≤ 4. Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<em>R</em> is transitive but not symmetric.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<em>R</em> is symmetric but not reflexive</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<em>R</em> is symmetric and transitive</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<em>R</em> is reflexive and transitive</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e20993577e5b242b745.png\" /></p>"},{"n":54,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a table ‘<em>T</em>’ in a relational database with key field ‘<em>K</em>’ A B-tree of order ‘<em>P</em>’ is used as an access structure on K where ‘P’ denotes the maximum number of tree pointer in a B-tree index node. Assume <em>K</em> is 12B long; disk block size is 1024 B; each data pointer <em>P<sub>D</sub></em> is 7 B long and each block pointer <em>P<sub>B</sub></em> is 6 B long. The maximum value of ‘P’ in order for each B-tree node to fit in a single disk block is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[41,41],"answerText":"41","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t41</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/07/07c766c16fe3f00c415f.png\" /></p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tFollowing is C like pseudo-code of a function that takes a queue as an argument and uses a stack S to do the processing.</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/67d45674739775fcf76a.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhat does the above function do in general?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Removes the last from Q"},{"key":"B","html":"Keeps the Q same as it was before the call"},{"key":"C","html":"Makes Q empty"},{"key":"D","html":"Reverses the Q"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)<br />\n\tThe function takes a queue Q as an argument. It dequeues all items of Q one by one and pushes them to a stack S one after another. So, stack contains all the elements of Q but in the reverse order of that in Q i.e. Front element of Q is now the bottom element of S and rear element of Q is now top element of S. Now we pop all items of S and enqueue the items back to Q. Since stack is<br />\n\tLIFO order, all items of queue are reversed.</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tThe subnet with 6 routers with delays shown in the following graph:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33b4a86e34ea9832d88f.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe distance vector routing algorithm is used. Router P has measured its routes to its neighbors Q,<br />\n\tR and S as 3, 7 and 2 respectively.<br />\n\tP has received the following vectors from node Q : (P, Q, R, S, T, U) [3, 0, 1, 4, 7, 5],<br />\n\tfrom node R : (P, Q, R, S, T, U) [7, 4, 0, 6, 8, 7] and<br />\n\tfrom node S : (P, Q, R, S, T, U) [2, 5, 7, 0, 4, 2].<br />\n\tWhich of the following is the new routing table at router P?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/5820f6dbd9278c59c1dc.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b8bbfebc68146cb3f76b.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b7/b74d0cf6313660d9c652.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b57dd787719db4d43ee8.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/6320af578c078658d17e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":57,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a diagonal matrix <em>A</em> = <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/413bb758df71bb0881da.png\" /> where <em>p</em>, <em>q</em> > 0. If the determinant |A| = 25, then the minimum value of the sum of eigen values <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/75/756e5b818effbf7dbbe2.png\" /> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[10,10],"answerText":"10","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t10</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d2fe321c5ebd3ae1bdc9.png\" /></p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If all edges in a graph have distinct weight then shortest path between two vertices is unique."},{"key":"B","html":"Removing one edge from spanning tree will not make the graph disconnected."},{"key":"C","html":"A complete graph with 4 vertices can have maximum 16 minimum cost spanning tree."},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tMST have <em>n</em> – 1 edges if graph has <em>n</em> vertices.</p>"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9afbc68231fe326e36cc.png\" /></p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tIdentify the correct statements:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"As we move away from the processors, the speed increases."},{"key":"B","html":"Temporal locality arises because of loops in a program."},{"key":"C","html":"Memory speedup can be achieved through the principle of locality of reference."},{"key":"D","html":"Spatial locality of reference arises because of sequential instruction execution."}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b, c, d)<br />\n\t&bull; When we move away from the processor, access time increases. For example: Cache access time is smaller than the secondary memory.<br />\n\t&bull; Temporal locality: It means that a instruction which is recently executed have high chances of execution again. This is mainly because we are executing same set of instructions again and again.<br />\n\t&bull; By increasing the principal of locality of reference, throughput and speed up is improved.<br />\n\t&bull; Spatial locality: It refers to the use of data elements within relatively class storage locations.</p>\n<p>\n\tSo, option (d) is also tree.</p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following functionalities must be implemented by a transport protocol over and above the network protocol?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Recovery from packet losses"},{"key":"B","html":"Detection of duplicate packets"},{"key":"C","html":"Packet delivery in the correct order"},{"key":"D","html":"End to end connectivity"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following CFG:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/239cdf7b29cc37c23a07.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich is the first and follow set of non-terminal D?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74db3bc5d0da5dfdd157.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a596ccb95ecf452b5953.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fcd2555355f2f28bf57a.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/aeee338e3e3dbf024305.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/60874c83ee145b0a848e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following program:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e21ce940ca794c34af1.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe value printed by the above program is _______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[44,44],"answerText":"44","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t44</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23ab08b87a5c864f5e4d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following, predicates for the domain of real numbers:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/198b89f0cf5b8a8789e0.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWhich of the following proposition gives true as the truth value?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee50df327756fbd4aa3d.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af52ac99845cdf9403e3.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/20bf513a0d5fa8782e64.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tA computer has a cache, main memory, and a disk used for virtual memory. If a referenced word is in the cache, 30 ns are required to access it. If it is in main memory but not in cache, 80 ns are needed to load it into the cache, and then the reference is started again. If the word is not in main memory, 22 ms are required to fetch the word from disk, followed by 80 ns to copy it to the cache and the start again. The cache hit ratio is 0.8 and main memory hit ratio is 0.9.<br />\n\tWhat is the approximate average time (μs) required to access a referenced word on this system?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"450"},{"key":"B","html":"520"},{"key":"C","html":"440"},{"key":"D","html":"480"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/340623d3dfec0b839d1b.png\" /></p>"},{"n":65,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a given cache line of 32 bytes. The bandwidth of main memory is 10 GB/s and latency is 64 ns. Find the time required to fetch the entire cache line from main memory ________ ns.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[67.2,67.3],"answerText":"67.2 [67.2 - 67.3]","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t67.2 [67.2 - 67.3]</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6ecc06476d6293763f46.png\" /></p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"NAT":17,"MCQ":38,"MSQ":10},"withoutKey":0}};
